From 27501b0951396422167433846645272d8d2beddc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 1aeo <github@1aeo.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 02:48:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 13/20] dos: Shed onion service descriptor fetches over a byte
 budget

Nothing bounds the descriptor bytes an HSDir answers per second. A
flood of fetches such as the one in tor#41339 takes as much as the
relay can serve, and every descriptor's clients compete with it.

Add HSDirFetchBudget, the onion service descriptor bytes an HSDir may be
asked for in a second before it sheds fetches: each fetch counts as the
size of the descriptor it asks for, whether or not it is answered. If it
is 0, the default, it is HSDirFetchBudgetPercent (a consensus parameter,
default 50) percent of the lower of the relay's configured and observed
bandwidth, and at least 512 KB. No fetch is refused until a second goes
over the budget. In the second after one over the budget, fetches still
within their descriptor's HSDirDescriptorFetchRate are answered first,
up to half the budget, whenever they arrive, and the others by a lottery
whose odds would have kept the previous second within it, up to the
budget, so a flood of a few descriptors is shed before the fetches of
the others. No second answers more than one and a half times the budget,
unless a single descriptor is larger than that; this also bounds a flood
that pauses every other second. The new includes for the bandwidth
estimates raise dos.c's practracker dependency-violation exception to 8.

A shed fetch closes its circuit with END_CIRC_REASON_RESOURCELIMIT,
without an answer, and is counted as action "refused" in
tor_relay_hsdir_fetch_total. Clients retry it at another of the
descriptor's HSDirs; a 503 would also make them avoid this relay for
other directory requests. Shedding is enforced only on public relays
with HSDirFetchShedding, an AUTOBOOL over the consensus parameter of the
same name (default 0). Either way, fetches while over the budget, and
those that are or would be shed, are counted as
tor_relay_dos_total{type="hsdir_fetch_pressure"} and
{type="hsdir_fetch_shed"}, so an operator can check a budget first. Once
shedding is enabled, the heartbeat logs the fetches selected for
shedding, which include any selected while it was off and answered.

Unlike refusing every fetch over a per-descriptor rate, this refuses
nothing until the relay goes over its budget, so denying a descriptor at
one HSDir takes about as many fetches as loading the relay. While it
sheds, whether a fetch is answered first still depends on the
descriptor's shared rate limiter, so someone who keeps the HSDir over
its budget can learn roughly how often a descriptor is fetched there. A
flood spread over many descriptors, each within its rate, shares the
first half of the budget with the fetches of the others, and the rest of
it goes to the lottery.

Part of #41339.
